-- =====================================================================
-- 0001_baseline.sql  —  PLACEHOLDER (this .EXAMPLE file is NOT applied)
-- =====================================================================
--
-- The real `0001_baseline.sql` is the initial schema snapshot generated by
-- `pg_dump --schema-only` of the live production database. It contains the
-- full set of tenant schemas (inventory_stg.*, employees_stg.*, analytics.*,
-- rest.*, etc.) and is therefore business-sensitive.
--
-- >>> The real baseline is NEVER committed to this public/open-core repo. <<<
--
-- How the real baseline is produced and applied:
--
--   1. Generate (private overlay repo / secure runner only):
--        pg_dump --schema-only --no-owner --no-privileges \
--          "$PROD_OR_STG_DATABASE_URL" > migrations/0001_baseline.sql
--
--   2. Sanitize: strip OWNER/GRANT lines and any environment-specific bits so
--      the file is additive-only DDL (CREATE ... / no DROP). The migration
--      runner (be/migrate.py) rejects DROP / ALTER ... DROP unless
--      SANMAI_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=1 is explicitly set.
--
--   3. Apply via the ledgered runner (records a row in schema_migrations):
--        python -m be.migrate \
--          --database-url "$DATABASE_URL" \
--          --dir ./migrations
--
-- All subsequent changes are additive, forward-only migration files
-- (0002_*.sql, 0003_*.sql, ...) applied in lexical order, each in its own
-- transaction and each ledgered in schema_migrations(version, name, checksum,
-- applied_at).
--
-- This .EXAMPLE file has the ".EXAMPLE" suffix specifically so the *.sql glob
-- in be/migrate.py does NOT pick it up. Do not rename it to end in ".sql".
-- =====================================================================
